### Testes de Cache Redis - ViaCEP
### Este arquivo testa a funcionalidade de cache para consultas de CEP

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5000
@token = seu_token_jwt_aqui

### 1. Login (obter token)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "admin@example.com",
  "password": "admin123"
}

###############################################################################
### TESTES DE CACHE HIT/MISS
###############################################################################

### 2. Primeira consulta - Cache MISS (deve consultar ViaCEP)
# Observe nos logs: "Cache MISS para CEP 01310100"
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "01310-100",
  "number": "1000",
  "complemento": "Teste Cache Miss"
}

### 3. Segunda consulta MESMO CEP - Cache HIT (deve recuperar do Redis)
# Observe nos logs: "Cache HIT para CEP 01310100"
# Esta chamada deve ser mais rápida que a anterior
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "01310-100",
  "number": "2000",
  "complemento": "Teste Cache Hit"
}

### 4. Terceira consulta MESMO CEP com formatação diferente - Cache HIT
# O CEP é normalizado, então "01310100" e "01310-100" são o mesmo no cache
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "01310100",
  "number": "3000",
  "complemento": "Teste Normalização"
}

###############################################################################
### TESTES DE ATUALIZAÇÃO COM CACHE
###############################################################################

### 5. Atualizar endereço com CEP já em cache - Cache HIT
# Substitua {addressId} pelo ID retornado na criação
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/address/1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": 1,
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "01310-100",
  "number": "1500",
  "complemento": "Atualizado via Cache"
}

### 6. Atualizar para CEP diferente - Cache MISS (se nunca foi consultado)
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/address/1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": 1,
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "20040-020",
  "number": "100",
  "complemento": "Rio de Janeiro - Centro"
}

### 7. Criar endereço com o NOVO CEP - Cache HIT (foi cacheado no passo anterior)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "20040-020",
  "number": "200",
  "complemento": "Outro endereço no Rio"
}

###############################################################################
### TESTES DE MÚLTIPLOS CEPs
###############################################################################

### 8. CEP São Paulo - Jardins (Cache MISS na primeira vez)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "01452-000",
  "number": "100"
}

### 9. CEP Belo Horizonte - Centro (Cache MISS na primeira vez)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "30130-100",
  "number": "50"
}

### 10. CEP Brasília - Asa Sul (Cache MISS na primeira vez)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "70040-020",
  "number": "200"
}

### 11. Repetir CEP São Paulo - Cache HIT
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "01452-000",
  "number": "150"
}

### 12. Repetir CEP Belo Horizonte - Cache HIT
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "30130-100",
  "number": "75"
}

###############################################################################
### TESTES DE FALLBACK E RESILIÊNCIA
###############################################################################

### 13. CEP inválido - NÃO deve ser cacheado
# Mesmo consultando novamente, sempre será um MISS (CEPs inválidos não são armazenados)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "00000-000",
  "number": "0",
  "logradouro": "Rua Manual",
  "bairro": "Bairro Manual",
  "localidade": "Cidade Manual",
  "uf": "SP"
}

### 14. Repetir CEP inválido - Ainda será MISS (não foi cacheado)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "00000-000",
  "number": "1",
  "logradouro": "Outra Rua Manual",
  "bairro": "Outro Bairro",
  "localidade": "Outra Cidade",
  "uf": "RJ"
}

###############################################################################
### TESTES DE PERFORMANCE
###############################################################################

### 15. Consulta direta ViaCEP (para comparar tempo de resposta)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/address/viacep/01310-100
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 16. Criação com cache (para comparar tempo de resposta)
# Compare o tempo de resposta deste com o passo 2 (primeira consulta)
# Este deve ser significativamente mais rápido
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/address
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": 1,
  "cep": "01310-100",
  "number": "9999"
}

###############################################################################
### OBSERVAÇÕES PARA TESTES
###############################################################################

# Como interpretar os logs:
#
# Cache MISS:
# - "Cache MISS para CEP 01310100"
# - "Consultando ViaCEP"
# - "CEP 01310100 armazenado no cache com expiração de 1440 minutos"
#
# Cache HIT:
# - "Cache HIT para CEP 01310100"
# - NÃO há log "Consultando ViaCEP"
#
# Performance esperada:
# - Cache MISS: ~200-500ms (depende da latência do ViaCEP)
# - Cache HIT: ~10-50ms (apenas consulta ao Redis local)
#
# Para limpar o cache e testar novamente:
# Execute no terminal: redis-cli FLUSHDB
# Ou no Redis CLI: FLUSHDB
#
# Para verificar chaves no cache:
# redis-cli KEYS "viacep:*"
#
# Para ver dados de um CEP específico:
# redis-cli GET "viacep:01310100"
#
# Para ver TTL de uma chave:
# redis-cli TTL "viacep:01310100"
